type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521843} :answer-field/id #uuid "770dec33-da36-590b-98e3-de009fe90cf3" :answer-field/key "$(1,2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92e10c0d-3823-528f-87a3-277e9336972f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521824} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cf240d-8708-5599-aca2-d261591ea7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521857}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521824} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9e874dd-4e18-58ae-af29-7f29c7c49fd9" :answer-field/key "$(0,4)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "638eb9ae-09f8-5fce-b7bc-2aac37c3181f" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $8 x y + 2 y^{2}=16 x^{2} + 48$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 2 y^{2}&=16 x^{2} + 48\\\\- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 16 x^{2} + 8 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 4 y)\\,i-(- 32 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\32 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=4x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=4x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2(4x)^{2}+8x(4x)-16x^{2}-48&=0\\\\48x^{2}-48&=0\\\\x^{2}&=1\\\\x&=\\pm 1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(1,4)$ and $(-1,-4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a57fa11-825b-5d72-897b-0173f47e260f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(4,-4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521766} {:answer/id #uuid "2165a269-3f7c-5aba-b78b-82d4e0f644ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-4,4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521770} {:answer/id #uuid "55c4f24c-6643-5605-81fb-7978b6d363b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-4,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521803} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1fa8ea-2503-52d0-b202-c7610a2fcdaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-4,-4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521809} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ccf363-a0bd-5a86-90cc-34fb5f55c5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-12,12)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521855}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521770} :answer-field/id #uuid "2283f6dd-22a1-5e6f-b61e-5ea5da67f7c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "714feb07-8211-5226-a523-87577689d617" :question/problem "At which point on the curve $8 x y + 4 y^{2}=- 4 x^{2} + 3 y - 12$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 4 y^{2}&=- 4 x^{2} + 3 y - 12\\\\4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 12&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 12$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 12)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 12)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 8 y - 3)\\,i-(8 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 8 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4(x+(-x))^{2}-3(-x)-(-12)&=0\\\\3x-(-12)&=0\\\\3x&=-12\\\\x&=-4\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-4,4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521815 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fca5786-6f95-5edc-89d7-025c97273124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(12,-12)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521757} {:answer/id #uuid "4d04ae77-6226-5ce7-885b-f80ba15e2fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(6,-6)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521794} {:answer/id #uuid "5564689e-d3ce-5a9f-a719-7eb39d492591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(6,6)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521801} {:answer/id #uuid "9771a166-dec6-517f-84ff-1a74c2c15214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(6,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521826} {:answer/id #uuid "c971a363-ecc3-50ee-89d0-484b756504a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-6,6)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521794} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef88e170-4022-53af-bd77-e7b6f6076c55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "76dd91ba-d91e-5c6c-a612-22de10fca4c2" :question/problem "At which point on the curve $8 x y + 4 y^{2}=- 4 x^{2} + 2 y + 12$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 4 y^{2}&=- 4 x^{2} + 2 y + 12\\\\4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 12&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 12$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 12)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 2 y - 12)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 8 y - 2)\\,i-(8 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 8 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4(x+(-x))^{2}-2(-x)-(12)&=0\\\\2x-(12)&=0\\\\2x&=12\\\\x&=6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(6,-6)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36239cce-902e-52a9-b49c-e42a6bc1c132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(3,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521783} {:answer/id #uuid "67333ad8-5fc4-5f22-abf5-77c6242dbc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-12,12)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521808} {:answer/id #uuid "9f7e2fdc-5342-51c3-9ca1-bc037dcd0169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-3,-3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521829} {:answer/id #uuid "aeabbe9c-35f7-56c8-9956-5f4b302d43d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-3,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521842} {:answer/id #uuid "fb340755-5a6e-525b-9533-8be7ec844986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521842} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac6736fd-7e64-5c2d-8cf8-ffbe9d4a8da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a3e58fdb-d0b9-56b1-bc61-4cc0137e18be" :question/problem "At which point on the curve $8 x y + 4 y^{2}=- 4 x^{2} + 4 y - 12$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 4 y^{2}&=- 4 x^{2} + 4 y - 12\\\\4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 4 y + 12&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 4 y + 12$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 4 y + 12)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 8 y - 4)\\,i-(8 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 8 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4(x+(-x))^{2}-4(-x)-(-12)&=0\\\\4x-(-12)&=0\\\\4x&=-12\\\\x&=-3\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-3,3)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c751557-77e8-5772-8224-bcd8b52ba3c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-5,5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521777} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9640d8-fe2b-5a2c-9bf2-1921afc58809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-5,-5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521786} {:answer/id #uuid "912504b1-f9c3-5d19-8f90-fc428ccbb006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(5,-5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521822} {:answer/id #uuid "ae59b4c0-93bd-5c71-bcd3-142c6d6eb995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-15,15)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521841} {:answer/id #uuid "ccac1d60-6b01-5598-82a2-30b9d00efbf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-5,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521777} :answer-field/id #uuid "a368d38c-2b11-5487-a14f-a13efc2b6541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7a17a4a-d80f-51f1-afa6-1faeaa5943f8" :question/problem "At which point on the curve $2 x y + y^{2}=- x^{2} + 3 y - 15$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2 x y + y^{2}&=- x^{2} + 3 y - 15\\\\x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 3 y + 15&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 3 y + 15$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 3 y + 15)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 2 x y + y^{2} - 3 y + 15)\\,j\\\\&=(2 x + 2 y - 3)\\,i-(2 x + 2 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 2 x - 2 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}1(x+(-x))^{2}-3(-x)-(-15)&=0\\\\3x-(-15)&=0\\\\3x&=-15\\\\x&=-5\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-5,5)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f4e1d8e-84d6-5a20-9c01-8ae98987f8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521811} {:answer/id #uuid "c087c404-166b-5d66-9df7-23a64d6373c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521847} :answer-field/id #uuid "01392b38-8c9f-501e-947f-a711423a8b27" :answer-field/key "$(2,2)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "355d6b08-c81d-5a24-8d9b-b5b309763df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521781} {:answer/id #uuid "b9468b67-fe8b-596a-b36c-8a9160644e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521844}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521781} :answer-field/id #uuid "068014f6-160c-5582-b4e9-027adae7c44d" :answer-field/key "$(0,4)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521749} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79fd7a02-7f40-548d-8d9e-67af6ec26c31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521818} {:answer/id #uuid "91d5a3b4-81a9-5029-bfc7-fff806ff92c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521823} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b48a7c4-4358-56a2-b9e1-abd8e70b08cb" :answer-field/key "$(1,4)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521754} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "204cec1a-d643-526b-9513-6282f7765087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521769} {:answer/id #uuid "ea7623f8-926b-589d-b4d2-d244bf6811a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521769} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c70ce8-f1f7-5bbc-adca-e3c29eed1cca" :answer-field/key "$(2,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521764} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37b9e260-94cc-5539-a762-ef0c781c432d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521784} {:answer/id #uuid "a312f786-15df-5b00-91b3-215f9c7e7a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521830} :answer-field/id #uuid "3de24a5f-af67-5551-918b-272cb93832bb" :answer-field/key "$(2,4)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521788} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25dc062e-44f5-5471-a146-179e82acfe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521772} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4d2807-65a8-535d-b28f-d716f496cbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521773}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521773} :answer-field/id #uuid "f01ae0ea-77c1-50d9-838c-8c96b77ae81a" :answer-field/key "$(-2,-4)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0a0a0dc-dc0e-505f-ae57-9fc7b81e15fb" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $4 x y + 2 y^{2}=4 x^{2} + 48$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j$?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4 x y + 2 y^{2}&=4 x^{2} + 48\\\\- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- 4 x^{2} + 4 x y + 2 y^{2} - 48)\\,j\\\\&=(4 x + 4 y)\\,i-(- 8 x + 4 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\8 x - 4 y&=0\\\\y&=2x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=2x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}2(2x)^{2}+4x(2x)-4x^{2}-48&=0\\\\12x^{2}-48&=0\\\\x^{2}&=4\\\\x&=\\pm 2\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding points are $(2,4)$ and $(-2,-4)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "610037d6-086f-5d33-9324-3c7f32410b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521937} {:answer/id #uuid "a79858de-382f-57c9-825b-961f950d7c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "864" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521945} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f9d585-8faa-5268-b12b-2fdcc64f3823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521946} {:answer/id #uuid "c916df35-42a4-5393-8da8-761af2b8912d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521951} {:answer/id #uuid "d00bfab5-9db9-5243-9802-7274abd50e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521937} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf6f4a09-acba-5c61-9646-13d702921ef7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "195941f2-4fd4-51d8-92ba-f2af4412ca9c" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D 3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(4,0)$, $(5,0)$, $(-3,4)$ and $(-4,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x + 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(4,0)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,0) \\\\ T^{-1}(5,0)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(5\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(5\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,0) \\\\ T^{-1}(-3,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,4) \\\\ T^{-1}(-4,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):4\\le u\\le 5,\\ 0\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/31/31a73ea464a3b414e81849dbcd7b5a8361544eaa22a55b895ceb231ca2c1b42c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=u - 2 v,\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta 3 u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta 3 u v^{2}\\,\\left|1\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{4}^{5}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{4}^{5}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{4}^{5} \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(4\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-\\left(8\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=288.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2566b226-78ba-5620-a2bb-20ed184b4f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{208}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521933} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b59d48-1162-5793-b1a3-916982210776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{416}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521953} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0e9667-6120-5443-8dc8-ac8fa4fa68ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521956} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c73d73-78d0-5721-ba8f-6cd192b7dbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{208}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521958} {:answer/id #uuid "f31dccd4-370d-54df-a144-7a97b4575e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{208}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521958} :answer-field/id #uuid "b62663b9-d0d4-5a1d-8fb7-e42be263d7fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b956245-4ce3-574c-9b58-4a7a33618b2d" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(\\frac{5}{3},1)$, $(\\frac{7}{3},1)$, $(\\frac{11}{3},3)$ and $(3,3)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3 x - 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(\\frac{5}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(3,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(\\frac{7}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(\\frac{11}{3},3)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(5,3) \\\\ T^{-1}(3,3)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(3\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(3,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):3\\le u\\le 5,\\ 1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/f9/f9dc752c5aeff34bc911428090212f09e0b3322041842726bd53dd7185563e7d.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u}{3} + \\frac{2 v}{3},\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{1}{3}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{3}\\int_{3}^{5}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{3}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{3}^{5}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{1}^{3}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{3}^{5} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(3\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(5\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(3\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(9\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{25}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{26}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{208}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68024dbb-1bfe-5c9c-8341-cf970e266470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521938} {:answer/id #uuid "93842ced-df27-5a75-9cb9-3b355c5b5e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521944} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e92fad-788a-510a-b0ae-0d2eb5183902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "768" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521950} {:answer/id #uuid "d8446a02-683a-5274-92ef-3f281b89cc8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521955} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a749be-c484-53a6-8783-233e2a5bdac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "384" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521957} :answer-field/id #uuid "19d9a1ec-429e-5986-afbd-3436f9c27469" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "51626741-0a75-50cc-9c49-7af7f678c9a6" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D 3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(2,0)$, $(4,0)$, $(-4,4)$ and $(-6,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x + 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(2,0)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(2,0) \\\\ T^{-1}(4,0)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,0) \\\\ T^{-1}(-4,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,4) \\\\ T^{-1}(-6,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-6\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-6\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(2,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):2\\le u\\le 4,\\ 0\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/2f/2f38ba39afcd37730bd37cbaca5b8a37feae8aa84f6f79be32a548aed4048482.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=u - 2 v,\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 3 y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta 3 u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta 3 u v^{2}\\,\\left|1\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}\\int_{2}^{4}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\int_{0}^{4}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{2}^{4}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{2}^{4} \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(2\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(8\\right)-\\left(2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(6\\right) \\\\ &=384.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b55f68-a14c-5917-a518-e01e1926cf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521935} {:answer/id #uuid "6b74584d-ea17-5c65-9042-2448a5c856f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521939} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e9eb44-f660-557a-9a90-0af29ce8af09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521959} {:answer/id #uuid "e827d35e-2ddc-5e34-9c37-8ed37aa9c3bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "420" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521960} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe8a328-58e7-55cc-8db6-8c0516177cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "630" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521959} :answer-field/id #uuid "23d77bf1-f3c9-5d26-97bb-0f5f25b40bd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8946d413-0692-5ea3-8e4d-01dfe830794b" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(2,1)$, $(4,1)$, $(-2,4)$ and $(-4,4)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x + 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(2,1)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(4,1)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(6,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(-2,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(6,4) \\\\ T^{-1}(-4,4)&=(\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(-4\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):4\\le u\\le 6,\\ 1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/64/642ee19399f1c37417b256bffc49b4b1458b83b7ea9d1f758e18cac7f1fa620f.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}1&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=1\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=u - 2 v,\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|1\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{4}^{6}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{4}^{6}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{1}^{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{4}^{6} \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(6\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(4\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(18\\right)-\\left(8\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=1\\cdot \\left(21\\right)\\cdot \\left(10\\right) \\\\ &=210.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0c6fa9-aade-53d1-abd5-5cb1c1bbefe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521928} {:answer/id #uuid "3748060b-856d-5d0b-97ae-459b4bd36b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{315}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521934} {:answer/id #uuid "6c92578a-fff4-5328-96e6-ec06e6681c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{105}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521940} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9e5a16-ac9b-581c-bf65-33d1385d0a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521941} {:answer/id #uuid "906ac085-5460-5a4d-85d7-d73344f3d71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{105}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521943}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521943} :answer-field/id #uuid "d646d279-52c1-531c-b6e3-5251737b2429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bf56e97a-0ba5-52a7-9ed0-519e4ee2c25e" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the quadrilateral region with vertices $(- \\frac{1}{3},1)$, $(\\frac{2}{3},1)$, $(- \\frac{4}{3},4)$ and $(- \\frac{7}{3},4)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe integrand\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\n$$\n\nsuggests that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=3 x + 2 y,\\qquad v=y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\nT^{-1}:(x,y)\\mapsto (u(x,y),v(x,y)).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nThe vertices of our domain $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T^{-1}(- \\frac{1}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(1,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(\\frac{2}{3},1)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,1) \\\\ T^{-1}(- \\frac{4}{3},4)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(4,4) \\\\ T^{-1}(- \\frac{7}{3},4)&=(\\left(3\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right),\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{7}{3}\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)) \\\\ &=(1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(u,v):1\\le u\\le 4,\\ 1\\le v\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/35/35d966f8a5a8e6e04353e202788230edbe6581fa7820e04fbe86406285c2194d.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\partial u/\\partial x&\\partial u/\\partial y\\\\\\partial v/\\partial x&\\partial v/\\partial y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=\\begin{vmatrix}3&2\\\\0&1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\ &=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\left(\\frac{\\partial(u,v)}{\\partial(x,y)}\\right)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\nSolving these affine equations also gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u}{3} - \\frac{2 v}{3},\\qquad y=v.\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D y^{2} \\left(3 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,\\mathrm{d}y&=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\iint_\\Delta u v^{2}\\,\\left|\\frac{1}{3}\\right|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}\\int_{1}^{4}u v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\,\\mathrm{d}v \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}v^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}v\\cdot \\int_{1}^{4}u\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{v^{3}}{3}\\right]_{1}^{4}\\cdot \\left[\\frac{u^{2}}{2}\\right]_{1}^{4} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{3}/3-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{3}/3\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(4\\right)^{2}/2-\\left(\\left(1\\right)^{2}/2\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(\\left(\\frac{64}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(8\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\left(21\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{105}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521978 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "456588e3-83cc-563f-9367-be792e8abc74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521975} {:answer/id #uuid "58e583f7-1a74-515f-a245-2588bfab496b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521981} {:answer/id #uuid "9b3c0bb0-f9ff-541a-bf94-17f32452b3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,⟨0,4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521988} {:answer/id #uuid "c1752b8d-9bc7-55a9-a78d-4ef64cc0e533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521992} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6e2c60-64d5-5b00-beeb-ba80f94b613b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,3,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521992} :answer-field/id #uuid "c28aa913-878b-5c7a-96c6-b445761c8e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521993}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bb06bb9-e449-5e9a-8e4e-1903c28e4441" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,3,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,3,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,3,-3).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,3,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x + 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y - 8\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,3,-3)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,3,-3)$ and passes through the point $(0,3,-3)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,∇f(0,3,-3)=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ebf572-f7e0-5064-b433-5e754ffa4151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,⟨-2,-4,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521968} {:answer/id #uuid "488546e4-15d3-5b99-859b-9bf647202e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,⟨-2,-4,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521976} {:answer/id #uuid "84b579e9-9396-5375-a1aa-9e672056b65e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,⟨-2,0,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521985} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba36e9d-1dd3-5777-8a5b-96b89bca0937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521986} {:answer/id #uuid "b209261e-e9e4-5b52-a82b-1415b31e95a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-3,5⟩+t\\,⟨-2,-4,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521976} :answer-field/id #uuid "23252cb7-9e83-5c0a-9ea4-7c9e7a8a7588" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6dd1fd09-3ea2-5abd-b9f4-b090d278c7fb" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,-3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-3,5)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-3,5).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-3,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x - 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y + 8\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,-3,5)&=\\left(-2\\right)i+\\left(-4\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨-2,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,-3,5)$ and passes through the point $(0,-3,5)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,∇f(0,-3,5)=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,⟨-2,-4,-1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e1ed12-df31-57d1-82bb-6106e39e18f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521966} {:answer/id #uuid "1de8c378-ba35-5b9b-8fcb-c2c137ed9fb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,-2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521970} {:answer/id #uuid "50a7485f-6811-5e77-b09e-a7a14f6345ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521979} {:answer/id #uuid "c62d10cc-5f9d-5376-b9ad-f047eea3d135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521994} {:answer/id #uuid "fc984d92-31d5-59c1-bc24-96439932949d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521994} :answer-field/id #uuid "b63c1f3a-6c19-54d8-a2af-776eeca36e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8bfc56db-f764-5bb9-b626-8a45b50e648a" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,-2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-2,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-2,-3).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x + 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y + 4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,-2,-3)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(-4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,-2,-3)$ and passes through the point $(0,-2,-3)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,∇f(0,-2,-3)=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "443b68ca-890a-5fb1-ab79-d702c59b5aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521974} {:answer/id #uuid "5768953d-f1c1-5d2c-893e-6ad0d8c70929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521980} {:answer/id #uuid "81784560-888a-501c-bd67-ea96c214e504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨2,2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521984} {:answer/id #uuid "a04cb268-b28e-5645-9269-d7673d2472e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521989} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2a388d-4d37-57ab-b051-f9eb084e224f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨0,4,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521996}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521974} :answer-field/id #uuid "127b177e-2e1b-5f33-a97b-b1261da408d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d14649f6-3c65-5d67-bc1a-d5fd911e8608" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right)=0$ at the point $(1,2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,2,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,2,-3).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x\\right)i+\\left(4 y - 4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\∇f(1,2,-3)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,2,-3)$ and passes through the point $(1,2,-3)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,∇f(1,2,-3)=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "038395c4-5ee5-5766-85c6-fa9ea1134efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨1,1,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521965} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbc31da-7a6b-5861-94ed-9fbdd3762ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,1,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,4,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521972} {:answer/id #uuid "3c610510-ba43-5bcc-8c6e-c8778ec510c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,1,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521973} {:answer/id #uuid "48bd41e5-df4f-5ec2-b3dd-6e6494238e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,1,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521977} {:answer/id #uuid "76b8c1ca-8da8-54f0-8d8a-39244f16536c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r=⟨0,1,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,0,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521983}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521977} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c2f2833-2ad3-5473-9dab-b7a1c1523f17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dda478ef-ed9a-5beb-8c10-38cd838f0525" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1,1).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x + 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,1,1)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(4\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,1,1)$ and passes through the point $(0,1,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,1,1⟩+t\\,∇f(0,1,1)=⟨0,1,1⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,-1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f1a2079-1fc1-5955-bbfb-db810024db1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522088} {:answer/id #uuid "7e097b3d-1787-51eb-8d0e-f6ddc058f730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{u}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522267} {:answer/id #uuid "9096297c-682f-58dc-8315-adc51907dab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{v}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{v}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522314} {:answer/id #uuid "942264b3-d374-50be-87f0-6ee36149aa06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522323} {:answer/id #uuid "b31ba118-b4ec-54ec-bb50-628c0cc67dda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}uv{e}^{w}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522386}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522323} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd889248-045c-5336-9e14-01871b41eb76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a7a1582-d778-5af4-af63-1b6090d14c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-39062" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\le \\frac{2}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{xy}\\le z\\le \\frac{2}{xy}\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=xyz,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\,{e}^{xyz}\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=xy,\\,w=xyz\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is \n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ yz & xz & xy\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =xy\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =xy(x-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{uv}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{e}^{xyz}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{e}^{w}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{uv}|\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{e}^{w}}{uv}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dbc4c08-f008-5e72-b85a-8f2b4179ee34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522152} {:answer/id #uuid "4cfefb36-0c70-50b6-aa6a-4945530149e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522185} {:answer/id #uuid "97e3dd6a-5cc0-5fa9-95f1-4e769dfdd8a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522330} {:answer/id #uuid "c921974f-fc45-5558-acc3-f2310130ec21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522438} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8b09ec-156b-5830-8ce7-eac0fbd892c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522330} :answer-field/id #uuid "e00110c2-7288-57e8-9508-75b52e302e96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c40cfd48-e992-514e-93b1-f5d1aa09838d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162128" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=3x+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{12}{1+(3x+2z)^{2}}\\,dV,\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\le 2x+y\\le \\frac{2}{\\pi },\\,\\,0\\le 3x+2z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=3x+2z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\le u\\le \\frac{2}{\\pi },\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 2\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{12}{1+(3x+2z)^{2}}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{12}{1+{v}^{2}}\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{4}{(1+{v}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\pi }^{2/\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{4}{(1+{v}^{2})}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/\\pi }^{2/\\pi }\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{(1+{v}^{2})}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{1/\\pi }^{2/\\pi }\\cdot {[4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{\\pi })\\cdot (4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "167c9635-d107-553c-80f2-728998f64f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{v}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522065} {:answer/id #uuid "208798f3-1213-54c2-9110-17dc3e4b81b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{uv}2v\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522093} {:answer/id #uuid "41084583-9221-587e-a746-0339cfa254c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522161} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6d471a-064f-5e70-bfd7-077a3c169ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{uv}2v\\operatorname{sinh}⁡uv\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522305} {:answer/id #uuid "f6cc9b29-5be4-54fe-8424-7c6e39a3e9a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{v}\\frac{u\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522161} :answer-field/id #uuid "4072ebe9-e356-5a8b-bdd6-bdedd4bc5d7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f849acac-b165-56ce-af0c-ee1d4893cc84" :question/math-academy-id "q-39056" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\le 2,\\,2\\le x+y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,v=x+y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nthe triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(x+y)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dV\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,v=x+y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps: \n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 4,\\,0\\le w\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -2y & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2x+2y \\\\  & =2(x+y) \\\\  & =2v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$ \n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+y)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u}{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡u}{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "187034c7-6c87-5c42-9dc7-1f516dfcbeb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{4 x + 5 y}\\,i+\\frac{5}{4 x + 5 y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524113} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7b2c3c-dad5-5ea4-a872-ce1ccf3c8f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{4 x + y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{4 x + y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524115} {:answer/id #uuid "502b508c-3ade-5a47-ba04-34857a3103f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4 x + 5 y}\\,i+\\frac{4}{4 x + 5 y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524119} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6fc39b-4ef7-5b10-9a80-fbdfff179372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4 x + 5 y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{4 x + 5 y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524121} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1e4881-aabc-50f7-b50f-0758f22752ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x + y}\\,i+\\frac{4}{x + y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524113} :answer-field/id #uuid "baa6754a-07de-5e45-b568-59a61cf9227b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1ea28d03-fccc-5031-9dd7-432a9072ff6e" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(4 x + 5 y \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\ln{\\left(4 x + 5 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{4}{4 x + 5 y} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\ln{\\left(4 x + 5 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{5}{4 x + 5 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=f_{x}(x,y)\\,i+f_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\ &= \\frac{4}{4 x + 5 y}\\,i+\\frac{5}{4 x + 5 y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c76f153-0b49-57db-b5cc-eea784a1592e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,i+4 y \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,j+z \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524118} {:answer/id #uuid "61e3ecd5-7d41-5bbe-af1c-fc78ba063e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\left(4 y + z\\right) \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,i+4 \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,j+\\left(x + 4 y\\right) \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524122} {:answer/id #uuid "c4603d86-4cc6-53cf-9b06-15035ef02806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,i+4 \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,j+z \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524127} {:answer/id #uuid "e15996e3-8dfc-5760-8950-2cca21b0f2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z \\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,i+4 \\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,j+x \\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524128} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc6997b-8a72-5f8f-8cad-c0e06747a9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,i+4 \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,j+x \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524133} :answer-field/id #uuid "5684a5a6-02af-57f7-91ea-abac705db63f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3266754d-3a6d-5bf4-b53d-4b662bce4ae1" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=z \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=4 \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial z}\\left(\\sin{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=x \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=f_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+f_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+f_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\ &= z \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,i+4 \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,j+x \\cos{\\left(x z + 4 y \\right)}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f4f022-3c40-5ef2-aed8-074e8335a6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2 x + 6 y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2 x + 6 y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524117} {:answer/id #uuid "698bb7de-bbac-54cb-aa3b-2b1b1d740286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{2 x + 6 y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{2 x + 6 y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524123} {:answer/id #uuid "99e6d00c-6cbf-533a-a9f9-7931acbfd7b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2 x + y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2 x + y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524125} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d52396-f197-5c9e-a3df-49f183913ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{x + y}\\,i+\\frac{2}{x + y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524131} {:answer/id #uuid "f5658b5d-6e07-523c-88df-3bdcd81b3503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{2 x + 6 y}\\,i+\\frac{6}{2 x + 6 y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524132} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecfe8ba1-2105-507d-94ee-559e4407000a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8e3fd408-b47b-5667-a4ab-da6a8c86196d" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\ln{\\left(2 x + 6 y \\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x + 6 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{2}{2 x + 6 y} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\ln{\\left(2 x + 6 y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{6}{2 x + 6 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=f_{x}(x,y)\\,i+f_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{2 x + 6 y}\\,i+\\frac{6}{2 x + 6 y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c6b2e1-f84c-576c-abcb-d9a8fe842d09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(16 x - 8 y)\\,i+(12 x - 6 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524144} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2d6953-32eb-5b84-952e-0fe7e38ff484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(16 x + 12 y)\\,i+(- 8 x - 6 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524156} {:answer/id #uuid "82a14ca9-6156-53ff-a807-126f932fa8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 20 y)\\,i+(20 x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524167} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0406a9-d228-55a8-a225-25b047aac598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(32 x + 4 y)\\,i+(4 x - 12 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524175} {:answer/id #uuid "beb6f1e3-1bcb-53d2-886d-ca85c879277c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4 x - 12 y)\\,i+(32 x + 4 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524182}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524175} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc542378-23c6-5f0d-a0d7-2fd9c737c526" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "56dccd14-220e-5139-aad6-1c3d9263c553" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =4 x + 3 y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(4)\\,i+(3)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =4 x - 2 y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(4)\\,i+(-2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(4 x - 2 y)((4)\\,i+(3)\\,j)+(4 x + 3 y)((4)\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =(4 x - 2 y)(4)i+(4 x - 2 y)(3)j+(4 x + 3 y)(4)i+(4 x + 3 y)(-2)j \\\\  & =(16 x - 8 y)i+(12 x - 6 y)j+(16 x + 12 y)i+(- 8 x - 6 y)j \\\\  & =(32 x + 4 y)\\,i+(4 x - 12 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36e183f3-6a67-5c16-9e23-ef10ececf338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 6 x - 2 y)\\,i+(6 x + 2 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524149} {:answer/id #uuid "511ca0af-e50e-5e8b-893f-12bbf8d9f4af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 8 y)\\,i+(8 x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524154} {:answer/id #uuid "aacd3c03-67de-503a-b3c3-1e38459c9de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 6 x + 6 y)\\,i+(- 2 x + 2 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524176} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f45f57-3302-5746-b510-260125a96661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4 x + 4 y)\\,i+(- 12 x + 4 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524178} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc90aec-cdb8-5523-a4f3-2ae3d600632d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- 12 x + 4 y)\\,i+(4 x + 4 y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524192} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d3daa57-4d92-56f1-9dc7-324353b715ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "617022f3-6d62-5e70-ba01-a98523aa95af" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =2 x - 2 y,\\quad ∇f(x,y)=(2)\\,i+(-2)\\,j \\\\ g(x,y) & =- 3 x - y,\\quad ∇g(x,y)=(-3)\\,i+(-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g∇f+f∇g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(- 3 x - y)((2)\\,i+(-2)\\,j)+(2 x - 2 y)((-3)\\,i+(-1)\\,j) \\\\  & =(- 3 x - y)(2)i+(- 3 x - y)(-2)j+(2 x - 2 y)(-3)i+(2 x - 2 y)(-1)j \\\\  & =(- 6 x - 2 y)i+(6 x + 2 y)j+(- 6 x + 6 y)i+(- 2 x + 2 y)j \\\\  & =(- 12 x + 4 y)\\,i+(4 x + 4 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "719a1d1a-12f8-5022-9387-d4a2458c0561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(- \\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x - 3} e^{y + 4} + \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x - 3} e^{y + 4})\\,i+(\\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x - 3} e^{y + 4} - \\left(x - 3\\right) e^{x - 3} e^{y + 4})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524165} {:answer/id #uuid "b92588a3-3fa2-54c2-83ba-e428830f5893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + y + 1})\\,i+(\\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(y + 5\\right) e^{x + y + 1})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524181} {:answer/id #uuid "c9802aa0-b1cb-55d1-b1a2-db8a145d6fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(y + 5\\right) e^{x + y + 1})\\,i+(\\left(x - 2\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x + y + 1})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524184} {:answer/id #uuid "d31f9ad0-9696-5bd1-a8fc-68d9debd7a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x - 3} e^{y + 4})\\,i+(\\left(x - 3\\right) e^{x - 3} e^{y + 4})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524188} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c2888c-5aef-520c-adff-53f362fa29ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x - 3} e^{y + 4})\\,i+(\\left(x - 3\\right) \\left(y + 4\\right) e^{x - 3} e^{y + 4})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524189}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524181} :answer-field/id #uuid "6595e956-1aae-5fc1-8097-0c7c2b14064a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a18b5f63-dc6e-5c60-bea1-6d3675dd37d9" :question/problem "What is the gradient of the product $f(x,y)h(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}$ and $h(x,y)=(y+4){e}^{(x-3)}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $h(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fh]=f\\,∇\\,h+h\\,∇\\,f\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-3){e}^{(y+4)})={e}^{(y+4)} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-3){e}^{(y+4)})=(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j={e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the partial derivatives of $h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y+4){e}^{(x-3)})=(y+4){e}^{(x-3)} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y+4){e}^{(x-3)})={e}^{(x-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇h(x,y) & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\text{ }j=(y+4){e}^{(x-3)}\\text{ }i+{e}^{(x-3)}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the product rule, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)h(x,y)] & =(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}((y+4){e}^{(x-3)}\\text{ }i+{e}^{(x-3)}\\text{ }j)+(y+4){e}^{(x-3)}({e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3){e}^{(y+4)}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(x-3)(y+4){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j+(y+4){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3)(y+4){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =((x-3)+1)(y+4){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }i+(x-3)(1+(y+4)){e}^{(x-3)+(y+4)}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
